The SQL SELECT DISTINCT Statement
The SELECT DISTINCT statement is used to return only distinct (unique) values.

In a table, a column may contain several duplicate values - and sometimes you want to list only the unique values.
SELECT DISTINCT(catid) from product;

========================================
The SQL WHERE Clause
The WHERE clause is used to filter records.

The WHERE clause is used to extract only those records that fulfill a specific condition.
SELECT* from product WHERE price >= 20000
=============================================
The SQL ORDER BY
The ORDER BY keyword is used to sort the result-set in ascending or descending order.

The ORDER BY keyword sorts the result-set in ascending order (ASC) by default.

SELECT * FROM `product` ORDER BY pname ASC;


========================================
The SQL AND Operator
The WHERE clause can contain one or many AND operators.

The AND operator is used to filter records based on more than one condition.
SELECT * FROM `product` WHERE catid=2 AND price>5000;


SELECT * FROM `product` WHERE catid=2 OR price >=5000;
SELECT * FROM `product` WHERE NOT catid=5;
==============================================

SELECT * FROM `product` LIMIT 1,10;
